                        "left": 0
                      },
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "type": "frame",
                          "id": "web-mobile-home-hot-4n",
                          "name": "vitalik.eth",
                          "layout": "none",
                          "x": 20,
                          "y": 20,
                          "width": 20,
                          "height": 20,
                          "fill": "$color.placeholder",
                          "clip": true,
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "id": "web-mobile-home-hot-4o",
                          "x": 48,
                          "y": 21.5,
                          "content": "@vitalik.eth",
                          "fontFamily": "Inter",
                          "fontSize": 14,
                          "fontWeight": "600",
                          "fill": "#12212B"
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "id": "web-mobile-home-hot-4p",
                          "x": 20,
                          "y": 54,
                          "content": "Doing a bit of a self-experiment.\n\nGoal: use my personal health and travel data to provide personalized diet and exercise recommendations for me, using frontier models but in a way that avoids leaking to them any private information.\n\nStrategy: use a local model (Qwen 3.8 Flash Next) to orchestrate, and use powerful remote models as a tool call to benefit from higher-level thinking and knowledge that the local model does not have.\n\nThree-layer approach to privacy:\n\nAvoid leaking personally identifiable information, or leaking my identity through writing style -> local model writes the queries to the frontier model, not me\nAvoid leaking who I am through the payment channel -> zkAPI\nAvoid leaking who I am through networking / IP -> Tor\n\nYou need all three (and finally we have all three, at least to some extent)\n\nA skill file teaches the local model when and how to construct minimally-data-revealing requests to remote models. Use zkAPI-wrapped-with-Tor as a CLI tool.\n\nAnd everything works! I got the recommendations back, info from frontier models helped to improve them.\n\nMain deficiencies:\n\n* Tor is really not optimized for request-by-request de-linking, which is the only form of network-layer privacy that really makes sense in today's world (long-running identifiers are too fragile). Probably not private enough, and latency 10-100x higher than it could be, at the same time.\n* The skill file's request construction strategies are definitely far from optimal.\n* Qwen 3.8 Flash Next is still too slow for comfort. It's comfortably running at 20-30 TPS, but it would only really start to feel fast at 100+\n* There is a tradeoff: the more careful you are about what data you give to a remote model, the less it can help you\n\nhttps://github.com/ethereum/zkapi/pull/1…",
                          "fontFamily": "Inter",
                          "fontSize": 15.2,
                          "fontWeight": "400",
                          "fill": "#12212B",
                          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                          "width": 327,
                          "lineHeight": 1.6
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "frame",
                          "id": "web-mobile-home-hot-4r",
